**Read:** [Recycled Concrete Aggregates: A Review — McNeil and Kang](../sources/Recycled-Concrete-Aggregates-McNeil-Kang.lingua#r_7RV5XYMDWNA5KPVRYC7052WV8E) in full and a complete underlying study; [Breviary Technical Ceramics — Verband der Keramischen Industrie](../sources/Breviary-Technical-Ceramics.lingua#r_7998JE61RKQF7VXXYK197KRBH4), chemistry/manufacturing; the earlier glass, ceramic and cementitious-material readings.

Crushing a cementitious composite changes particle size and liberation but does not undo hydration. Adhered mortar and microcracking can change absorption, stiffness and mixing-water demand. Recovered glass may carry incompatible ceramics, colors or surface contamination; a refractory can retain reaction products from service. A recovered mass is not automatically equivalent to virgin feed.

Compare quantities on the basis relevant to function. Equal mass substitution can change volume, grading or water balance. A material test and a whole-member test answer different questions. Source, conditioning, mix proportions and curing must remain visible when comparing results.

**Work:** Reconstruct a recovered-aggregate comparison, including absorbed versus effective mixing water and the basis of replacement. Reconcile specimen response with a component observation. Then contrast it with a glass or ceramic recovery route whose transformations differ. Explain what must be characterized before claiming suitability for the next use.

**Return:** [Materials engineering — structure, processing and useful behavior](../Materials-engineering.lingua#r_05BF5JM8141RKF1VT5998YFZQ9).
